{"from": "site:samaria-sebaste", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:ac-08-05-city-samaria-sebaste", "grade": "A", "note": "The excavated city was refounded and renamed Sebaste by Herod the Great in the late 1st century BC (fetched Samaria (ancient city) article; Harvard Expedition 1908-1910 and Joint Expedition 1931-1935): A for the place Philip's 'city of Samaria' names."}
{"from": "site:samaria-sebaste", "type": "attests", "to": "place:samaria", "grade": "A", "note": "A source names the same place: the excavated tell of Samaria/Sebaste, Herod's refounded city."}
{"from": "site:samaria-sebaste", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:ac-08-05-philip-preached-christ-samaria", "grade": "B", "note": "Confirms the setting only: a major urban centre stood at Samaria/Sebaste in the Herodian-Roman period. It does not attest that Philip preached there."}
{"from": "site:caesarea-maritima", "type": "attests", "to": "place:caesarea", "grade": "A", "note": "A source names the same place: the excavated city of Caesarea Maritima, built by Herod the Great and dedicated to Augustus, seat of the Roman governors of Judea."}
{"from": "site:caesarea-maritima", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:ac-08-40-philip-azotus-caesarea", "grade": "B", "note": "Confirms the setting only: Caesarea was a major city on the coast in the period, the end point of Philip's preaching route. It does not attest that Philip preached there."}
{"from": "artifact:great-isaiah-scroll", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:ac-08-32-33-isaiah-53-7-8-read", "grade": "B", "note": "The pre-Christian Great Isaiah Scroll (c. 125 BCE) contains the entire Book of Isaiah, including the quoted Isaiah 53:7-8, as the Great Isaiah Scroll carried for Gospel quotations (calibration rule B). It attests the pre-Christian form of the quoted passage, not the reading event."}
{"from": "text:strabo-geography-17-1-54", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:ac-08-27-candace-kandake-title", "grade": "A", "note": "Strabo (writing under Augustus/Tiberius) names 'Candace, queen of the Ethiopians in our time' - the same title formula as Acts 8:27 for a Meroitic ruler (usually identified as Amanirenas, c. 25 BCE), a generation before Acts. It names the title/office, not the specific queen of the eunuch."}
{"from": "text:strabo-geography-17-1-54", "type": "attests", "to": "place:kush-meroe", "grade": "B", "note": "Strabo's Candace rules the Ethiopians beyond Egypt, with her royal seat at Napata in the Meroitic heartland - the kingdom of Kush in the same period, not necessarily the named place alone."}
{"from": "text:pliny-nh-6-186-candace", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:ac-08-27-candace-kandake-title", "grade": "A", "note": "Pliny reports that at Meroe 'a female, whose name was Candace, ruled over the district, that name having passed from queen to queen for many years' - Candace as a dynastic title of the queens of Kush, exactly the Acts usage."}
{"from": "text:pliny-nh-6-186-candace", "type": "attests", "to": "place:kush-meroe", "grade": "B", "note": "Pliny locates the Candace's rule at the island of Meroe, confirming the polity and capital in the period."}
{"from": "text:josephus-ant-16-eunuchs", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:ac-08-27-eunuch-treasurer", "grade": "C", "note": "Context only: Josephus shows eunuchs held trusted posts in a first-century Judaean royal court (Herod's). A parallel from another court; it does not attest this eunuch or the Kandake's court."}
{"from": "text:josephus-ant-20-7-2-simon", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:ac-08-09-simon-sorcerer", "grade": "D", "note": "Disputed identification: Josephus names a magician Simon, a Jew of Cyprus and friend of the procurator Felix, who persuaded Drusilla (Ant. 20.7.2; 'Atomus' in Latin manuscripts). Some scholars identify him with Simon Magus; this is not generally accepted, since the Josephus Simon is a Jew, not a Samaritan (fetched Wikipedia Simon Magus article)."}
{"from": "text:justin-first-apology-26", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:ac-08-09-simon-sorcerer", "grade": "D", "note": "Reception: Justin, a Samaritan of Flavia Neapolis writing c. 155, reports that a Samaritan Simon of Gitto did mighty acts of magic in Claudius's Rome and was honoured with a statue on the Tiber, and that almost all Samaritans venerated him. A reception tradition, D per calibration; Justin reports a cult still current in his own day, but the report is a tradition."}
{"from": "text:justin-first-apology-26", "type": "attests", "to": "person:simon-magus", "grade": "D", "note": "Reception: Justin names the same man (Simon the Samaritan magician) and reports his veneration; graded D as a reception text reporting a tradition."}
{"from": "text:irenaeus-against-heresies-3-12-8", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:ac-08-36-38-eunuch-baptized", "grade": "D", "note": "Reception: Irenaeus (c. 180) names the eunuch ('Simeon Bachos') and reports that he was sent to preach in Ethiopia what he had believed - the earliest patristic witness to the episode, a tradition, not independent evidence."}
{"from": "artifact:semo-sancus-inscription", "type": "complicates", "to": "person:simon-magus", "note": "The statue that Justin said bore 'Simoni Deo Sancto' (to Simon the holy god) was, when unearthed on the Tiber island in the 16th century, found to be inscribed to the Sabine deity Semo Sancus ('Semoni Sanco Deo', Gruter vol. 1 p. 95 n. 5); scholars conclude Justin confused Semoni Sancus with Simon (fetched Wikipedia Simon Magus article)."}
